{
  "abstract": "Exact-two binary refinement replaces every point $s$ of a no-three-in-line set by exactly two children in $2s+\\{0,1\\}^2$. It preserves density, so a seed above $1.8$ that refined forever would solve the no-three-in-line target on the sides $n_02^j$. Paper 47 shows that such a refinement would need a purely unrectifiable limiting support, but leaves open whether any indefinitely refinable parent exists. We prove that none does: no seed of any size or density admits more than six generations, and six is attained from a single point. The proof reduces every lineage of a refinement chain to a chain from one point. An exact computation then shows that one-point chains stop at depth six. We enumerate the 1,326 dihedral classes at level three with two independent enumerators. 1,323 classes cannot be extended three more levels, and the three survivors cannot be extended four. Each decision is made by CaDiCaL and, independently, by CP-SAT on a separately written model, and DRAT refutations are produced for the deepest cases. A verified 64-point chain at side 64 attains the bound. A finite study explains where chains end. Every listed $2n$ set with $3\\le n\\le17$, $n\\ne4$, fails to double once. The lines that first block a chain have primitive height $2$–$7$, although unrestricted sets reach $2n$ under such short lines. Widening the child window to $\\{-1,\\dots,2\\}^2$ moves the blocking height to 6, and one-point chains then reach depth at least seven. The theorem covers exactly this fixed-window class. Wider fixed windows, windows growing with depth, and the growing-displacement transport of paper 23 remain open.",
